1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Eight numbers. Find the largest. The only operation you may use compares two of them.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
A growth rate hides the constant. A count does not. GATE asks for the count.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
The counter starts at zero. Every comparison below is paid for, and none is free.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Three jobs, three different counts: the largest alone, the largest and smallest together, and the second largest.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Scan once, keeping the best so far: seven comparisons for eight numbers.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Every comparison knocks one candidate out of the race. Seven knockouts leave one standing.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Stop after six comparisons and two candidates are still undefeated. The adversary says either could be the largest.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
So n minus 1 is exact: achievable and unavoidable. That pairing of words is what the rest of the lesson repeats.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Now find both the smallest and the largest. The obvious method scans twice.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Two scans cost two lots of n minus 1, which is 2n minus 2. At n = 100 that is 198.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Look at what one comparison tells you: of two numbers, one is a candidate for the largest and the other for the smallest.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:32,000
So the naive count is not the minimum. It is one comparison per pair too many, and the next scene collects that.

13
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
First compare the numbers in pairs. Fifty comparisons at n = 100, and every pair is now ordered.

14
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Now the largest must be among the fifty larger members. Finding it costs 49 comparisons.

15
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:56,000
And the smallest must be among the fifty smaller members: another 49 comparisons.

16
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Fifty plus 49 plus 49 is 148. In general floor(n/2) plus 2 times ceil(n/2) minus 2, which is 3n/2 minus 2 for even n.

17
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:14,000
To prove no method beats 148, track each element in one of four states.

18
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:22,000
At the end, one element has only won, one has only lost, and the other n minus 2 have both.

19
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:30,000
A comparison between two untouched elements pays two units. Every other comparison pays at most one.

20
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:40,000
At most floor(n/2) comparisons can be generous, so at least 2n minus 2 minus floor(n/2) comparisons are needed: ceil(3n/2) minus 2.

21
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:48,000
With an odd count one element is left over after pairing. Compare it to both champions: two more comparisons.

22
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:56,000
One formula covers both parities: ceil(3n/2) minus 2. Two numbers: 1, and three numbers: 3.

23
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:04,000
The table for n from 2 to 12: naive 2n minus 2, the pairing count, and the formula. They agree on every row.

24
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,000
The saving over the naive method is exactly floor(n/2): one comparison for each pair.

25
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
The second largest. The obvious method: find the largest, then find the largest of the rest.

26
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
At n = 8 that is 13 comparisons. The answer is 9, and the gap is the whole lesson.

27
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Think about who can be second. Only someone who lost directly to the largest can be.

28
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:40,000
So the second search does not need the whole array. It needs one list: the elements the largest beat.

29
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Eight leaves, four matches, then two, then one: seven comparisons to find the largest.

30
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:56,000
The winner played three matches. Its three victims are the only candidates for second place.

31
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Find the largest of those three: two comparisons. Total 7 plus 2 is 9.

32
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:12,000
The saving over 2n minus 3 is n minus 1 minus ceil(log2 n). At n = 8 that is 4, and it grows.

33
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Why can no method use fewer than n plus log n minus 2? Count what the winner must have beaten.

34
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:28,000
If the winner beat only two elements, either of the others could be second and the adversary may choose it.

35
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:36,000
So the winner must beat at least ceil(log2 n) elements, and each must be compared against the others: ceil(log2 n) minus 1 more.

36
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
The same argument gives the maximum alone as n minus 1, and the pair as ceil(3n/2) minus 2: three counts, three proofs.

37
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Do not take the formulas on trust. Search every comparison strategy for two, three, four and five numbers.

38
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:58,000
The answers are 1, 3, 4 and 6 for both jobs: the same numbers the formulas give.

39
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:06,000
For n up to 6 the two formulas coincide: 1, 3, 4, 6, 7, 7. At n = 7 they part: 9 against 8.

40
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:12,000
The search is only a check. The proof is the adversary, and the two agree wherever both apply.

41
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:20,000
GATE CSE 2014 Set 1, question 39: the minimum comparisons to find the minimum and maximum of 100 numbers.

42
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:28,000
n = 100 is even, so the count is 3n/2 minus 2, which is 148. The published key is 148, accepted over 147.1 to 148.1.

43
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:38,000
GATE CSE 2021 Set 1, question 2: t is the lowest upper bound on the comparisons. Answer: t is above n and at most 3 ceil(n/2).

44
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Four steps: name the facts the answer needs, count what one comparison can deliver, divide, then exhibit the algorithm that attains it.

